-- #1620 — RE-POSED ONTO THE CROSS-MODULE AXIS (2026-08-15). Two interfaces, declared in
-- two DIFFERENT modules, declare the SAME method name `ping` at DIFFERENT return types,
-- both implemented on one concrete type `T`. `check` accepts the program at exit 0 and the
-- BUILT BINARY EXITS 0 DELIVERING A VALUE OF THE OTHER TYPE.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY IT MOVED. The previous shape put both interfaces in THIS file. The Q1 unit
-- (Stage B / Phase 4b) now rejects that on the declaration (R-DUPLICATE-IFACE-METHOD), so
-- the old fixture stopped reaching the defect it pins and the must-fail gate reported it
-- DRAINED. #1620 is NOT fixed by Q1: Q1 covers only the INTRA-module half --
-- `duplicateErrors` runs inside `buildErrors`, which has no import table at all -- so
-- nothing in it can see two interfaces that meet only through an `import`. That is where
-- this bug now lives, and this file is it.
--
-- ⭐ HAND-DERIVED FROM THIS FILE'S OWN IMPORTS AND ITS OWN TWO INTERFACES. The expected
-- value is NOT inherited from the shape this replaces, and must not be justified by citing
-- it -- that fixture is gone and a pin whose reasoning points at a deleted file cannot be
-- audited. The derivation, from scratch:
--
--   1. `import beta.{Beta, ping}` binds the METHOD NAME `ping`. `import alpha.{Alpha}`
--      binds only an INTERFACE NAME. So the occurrence `ping T` below has EXACTLY ONE
--      binding in scope: `Beta.ping : a -> Int`. There is no second denotation to choose
--      between, and therefore no name-resolution question to settle.
--   2. Hence `ping T : Int`, and `ping T == 7` is a well-typed Int comparison. Shown
--      directly rather than assumed -- replacing the comparand with a String makes `check`
--      report `Type mismatch: Int vs String` (measured), i.e. the front end really did
--      resolve the occurrence to Beta.
--   3. The only impl of `Beta` at `T` is `impl Beta T where ping _ = 7`, in this file.
--   4. ⇒ `ping T` is 7, `ping T == 7` is True, and THIS PROGRAM MUST PRINT True.
--
-- Printing False means the program delivered `impl Alpha T`'s body -- the String "alpha" --
-- where the type it was ACCEPTED at says Int. A PROGRAM THE COMPILER ACCEPTS MUST DELIVER A
-- VALUE OF THE TYPE IT ACCEPTED IT AT; that is violated here with no diagnostic on any verb.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE PROJECTION AND NOT THE RAW VALUE. `main` compares to 7 rather than printing
-- `ping T`: the raw form's built binary prints a LIVE HEAP ADDRESS, different on every
-- execution, so an exact-match pin on it would red on the next run for a reason unrelated
-- to this bug. raw-probe.mdk carries that channel and is not graded.
--
-- ⇒ EVERY FIX DIRECTION DRAINS THIS ROW:
--   * select by interface identity -> `ping T` is 7 -> this prints True -> stdout moves;
--   * reject the cross-module collision -> `medaka build` exits 1 -> the build-run verb
--     returns a hard 126 -> the exit assertion fails.
import beta.{Beta, ping}
import alpha.{Alpha}

data T = T

impl Alpha T where
  ping _ = "alpha"

impl Beta T where
  ping _ = 7

main = println (ping T == 7)
